{
 "id": "14.2",
 "module": "m68804",
 "chapter": "14",
 "title": "pH and pOH",
 "short": "pH and pOH",
 "lead": "The concentrations of hydronium and hydroxide ions in water span many orders of magnitude. This section expresses them on the logarithmic pH and pOH scales, uses those scales to describe a solution as acidic, basic, or neutral, and shows how the pH of a solution is measured.",
 "objectives": [
  "Explain the characterization of aqueous solutions as acidic, basic, or neutral",
  "Express hydronium and hydroxide ion concentrations on the pH and pOH scales",
  "Perform calculations relating pH and pOH"
 ],
 "summary_html": "<p>Concentrations of hydronium and hydroxide ions in aqueous media are often represented as logarithmic pH and pOH values, respectively. At <span data-type=\"temperature\">25 °C</span>, the autoprotolysis equilibrium for water requires the sum of pH and pOH to equal <span data-type=\"equilibrium-constant\">14</span> for any aqueous solution. The relative concentrations of hydronium and hydroxide ion in a solution define its status as acidic ([H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>] &gt; [OH<sup>−</sup>]), basic ([H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>] &lt; [OH<sup>−</sup>]), or neutral ([H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>] = [OH<sup>−</sup>]). At <span data-type=\"temperature\">25 °C</span>, a pH &lt; 7 indicates an acidic solution, a pH &gt; 7 a basic solution, and a pH = 7 a neutral solution.</p>",
 "notes": "Nothing of the section is left out. Kept as printed: Figure 14.3’s caption “It also is corrodes statues”, and the key to the last exercise, which gives the hydronium ion concentration the question asks for under the label [OH⁻].",
